questionText:
Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2,                     // corePoolSize
        5,                     // maximumPoolSize
        60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

PoolState.print("before any submit", executor);
for (int i = 1; i <= 2; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

Actual output:

    before any submit              poolSize=0  active=0  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=0
    after submitting task 1        poolSize=1  active=1  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=1
    after submitting task 2        poolSize=2  active=2  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=2
    ...
    after termination              poolSize=0  active=0  queued=0  completed=2  everScheduled=2

corePoolSize=2, maximumPoolSize=5. Exactly 2 tasks are submitted. How many worker threads exist once both are running?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2,                     // corePoolSize
        5,                     // maximumPoolSize
        60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

PoolState.print("before any submit", executor);
for (int i = 1; i <= 2; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

Same pool (core=2, max=5, queue capacity 3). A THIRD task is now submitted while the first two are still running. Does a third worker get created for it?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each SleepTask sleeps 3 seconds
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

Given the previous question's result, what condition would actually have to be true for the pool to create a THIRD worker?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each SleepTask sleeps 4 seconds
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

Same config (core=2, max=5, queue capacity 3). Eight 4-second tasks are submitted in a row. Walk through what happens to tasks 6, 7 and 8 specifically.

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each SleepTask sleeps 4 seconds
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

Once all 8 tasks in the previous question have been submitted, which finish FIRST: tasks 3-5 (queued early) or tasks 6-8 (given new workers)?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each SleepTask sleeps 4 seconds
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

core=2, max=6 this time, queue capacity 4. If 20 identical slow tasks are submitted all at once, what is the LARGEST poolSize can ever reach?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each SleepTask sleeps 4 seconds
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

core=3, max=3 (deliberately equal), queue capacity 5. Ten tasks are submitted. What happens to poolSize as they arrive?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each SleepTask sleeps 4 seconds
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

Following the previous question (core=3, max=3, queue capacity 5): what happens to task number 9?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2,                     // corePoolSize
        5,                     // maximumPoolSize
        60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

PoolState.print("before any submit", executor);
for (int i = 1; i <= 2; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

A pool is built with corePoolSize=4 but only 1 task is ever submitted to it in the program's whole lifetime. How many worker threads does the pool actually create?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2,                     // corePoolSize
        5,                     // maximumPoolSize
        60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

PoolState.print("before any submit", executor);
for (int i = 1; i <= 2; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each SleepTask sleeps 3 seconds
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

Comparing experiment 1 (2 tasks) and experiment 2 (5 tasks) on the identical core=2/max=5/queue=3 pool: why does poolSize end at 2 in BOTH cases, despite very different task counts?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each SleepTask sleeps 4 seconds
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

core=2, max=5, ArrayBlockingQueue(3). Six tasks are submitted. Predict poolSize and queued once all six have been accepted.

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each SleepTask sleeps 4 seconds
    PoolState.print("after submitting task " + i, executor);
}

Same setup as the previous question. What is poolSize + queued equal to at every single point during that submission sequence, from task 1 through task 6?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3),
        new ThreadPoolExecutor.AbortPolicy());

for (int i = 1; i <= 9; i++) {
    try {
        executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));    // each sleeps 5 seconds
        PoolState.print("accepted task " + i, executor);
    } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
        Log.line("!!! Task " + i + " REJECTED -> " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
    }
}

Actual output:

    accepted task 1                poolSize=1  active=1  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=1
    accepted task 2                poolSize=2  active=2  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=2
    ...
    accepted task 8                poolSize=5  active=5  queued=3  completed=0  everScheduled=8
    !!! Task 9 REJECTED -> RejectedExecutionException

core=2, max=5, queue capacity 3, AbortPolicy. What is the total number of tasks this pool can be holding (running + queued) at once, before a 9th is rejected?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3),
        new ThreadPoolExecutor.AbortPolicy());

for (int i = 1; i <= 9; i++) {
    try {
        executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));    // each sleeps 5 seconds
        PoolState.print("accepted task " + i, executor);
    } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
        Log.line("!!! Task " + i + " REJECTED -> " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
    }
}

Actual output:

    accepted task 1                poolSize=1  active=1  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=1
    accepted task 2                poolSize=2  active=2  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=2
    ...
    accepted task 8                poolSize=5  active=5  queued=3  completed=0  everScheduled=8
    !!! Task 9 REJECTED -> RejectedExecutionException

Which THREE conditions must ALL be true at once for a task to be rejected?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3),
        new ThreadPoolExecutor.AbortPolicy());

for (int i = 1; i <= 9; i++) {
    try {
        executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));    // each sleeps 5 seconds
        PoolState.print("accepted task " + i, executor);
    } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
        Log.line("!!! Task " + i + " REJECTED -> " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
    }
}

core=2, max=5, queue capacity 3, AbortPolicy. If the queue capacity were changed to 6 instead, how many tasks could the pool accept before rejecting one?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
