Is it possible for a RejectedExecutionException to be thrown even while poolSize is still below maximumPoolSize?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5,
        5, TimeUnit.SECONDS,          // keepAliveTime
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each sleeps 2 seconds
}
PoolState.print("right after the burst", executor);
// ... nothing else submitted for the next 12+ seconds ...

What is keepAliveTime actually measuring?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5,
        5, TimeUnit.SECONDS,          // keepAliveTime
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each sleeps 2 seconds
}
PoolState.print("right after the burst", executor);
// ... nothing else submitted for the next 12+ seconds ...

core=2, max=5, keepAliveTime=5 seconds. A burst of 8 short tasks forces poolSize up to 5. Once all the work is done and nothing new is submitted, what happens to poolSize over the following 20 seconds?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5,
        5, TimeUnit.SECONDS,          // keepAliveTime
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each sleeps 2 seconds
}
PoolState.print("right after the burst", executor);
// ... nothing else submitted for the next 12+ seconds ...

Why does the pool bother creating those 3 extra workers in experiment 6 at all, if they are just going to time out and disappear 5 seconds later?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5,
        5, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

executor.allowCoreThreadTimeOut(true);

for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each sleeps 1 second
}
// ... nothing else submitted afterwards ...

What does allowCoreThreadTimeOut(true) change about the behaviour just described?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5,
        5, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

executor.allowCoreThreadTimeOut(true);

for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each sleeps 1 second
}
// ... nothing else submitted afterwards ...

Compare experiment 6 (no core timeout) and experiment 7 (allowCoreThreadTimeOut(true)), both with keepAliveTime=5s, after all work is done and 20 idle seconds pass. What is poolSize in each?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5,
        5, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

executor.allowCoreThreadTimeOut(true);

for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
