}
// ... nothing else submitted afterwards ...

The Javadoc for allowCoreThreadTimeOut states it requires keepAliveTime to be greater than zero. Why would a keepAliveTime of 0 combined with this flag not make sense?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5,
        5, TimeUnit.SECONDS,          // keepAliveTime
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each sleeps 2 seconds
}
PoolState.print("right after the burst", executor);
// ... nothing else submitted for the next 12+ seconds ...

keepAliveTime=5 seconds. An extra (non-core) worker finishes a task at second 10 and immediately picks up ANOTHER waiting task at second 10.3. Does the keepAliveTime clock ever get a chance to expire for that worker?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5,
        5, TimeUnit.SECONDS,          // keepAliveTime
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each sleeps 2 seconds
}
PoolState.print("right after the burst", executor);
// ... nothing else submitted for the next 12+ seconds ...

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5,
        5, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));

executor.allowCoreThreadTimeOut(true);

for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));   // each sleeps 1 second
}
// ... nothing else submitted afterwards ...

Why did both experiment 6 and experiment 7 use a small keepAliveTime (5 seconds) rather than the default-ish 60 seconds used in experiments 1-5?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new LinkedBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));    // capacity 3 = bounded, same class as experiment 8

for (int i = 1; i <= 9; i++) {
    try {
        executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));       // each sleeps 5 seconds
        PoolState.print("accepted task " + i, executor);
    } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
        Log.line("!!! Task " + i + " REJECTED");
    }
}

Actual output:

    accepted task 1     poolSize=1  active=1  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=1
    accepted task 2     poolSize=2  active=2  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=2
    accepted task 3     poolSize=2  active=2  queued=1  completed=0  everScheduled=3
    accepted task 4     poolSize=2  active=2  queued=2  completed=0  everScheduled=4
    accepted task 5     poolSize=2  active=2  queued=3  completed=0  everScheduled=5
    accepted task 6     poolSize=3  active=3  queued=3  completed=0  everScheduled=6
    accepted task 7     poolSize=4  active=4  queued=3  completed=0  everScheduled=7
    accepted task 8     poolSize=5  active=5  queued=3  completed=0  everScheduled=8
    !!! Task 9 REJECTED - pool at max and queue full

Experiment 9 uses the exact same class, new LinkedBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3), just WITH a capacity number this time. Compare its behaviour to experiment 8's.

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new LinkedBlockingQueue<Runnable>());     // NO capacity given -> unbounded

for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));           // each sleeps 2 seconds
}

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new LinkedBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3));    // capacity 3 = bounded, same class as experiment 8

for (int i = 1; i <= 9; i++) {
    try {
        executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));       // each sleeps 5 seconds
        PoolState.print("accepted task " + i, executor);
    } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
        Log.line("!!! Task " + i + " REJECTED");
    }
}

Given experiments 8 and 9 use the identical LinkedBlockingQueue class but behave completely differently, what is the actual variable that determines ThreadPoolExecutor's behaviour?

Real code:

private static void runWorkload(String label, BlockingQueue<Runnable> queue) {
    ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
            2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS, queue);
    int accepted = 0, rejected = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 9; i++) {
        try {
            executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));
            accepted++;
        } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
            rejected++;
        }
    }
    Log.line(label + " accepted=" + accepted + "  rejected=" + rejected);
}

// called once with new ArrayBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3)
// called once with new LinkedBlockingQueue<Runnable>(3)

Experiment 10 runs the identical 9-task workload through ArrayBlockingQueue(3) and LinkedBlockingQueue(3) separately. Predict whether the accepted/rejected counts differ between the two.

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new SynchronousQueue<Runnable>());

for (int i = 1; i <= 7; i++) {
    try {
        executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));     // each sleeps 5 seconds
        PoolState.print("accepted task " + i, executor);
    } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
        Log.line("!!! Task " + i + " REJECTED");
    }
}

Actual output:

    accepted task 1     poolSize=1  active=1  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=1
    accepted task 2     poolSize=2  active=2  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=2
    accepted task 3     poolSize=3  active=3  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=3
    accepted task 4     poolSize=4  active=4  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=3
    accepted task 5     poolSize=5  active=5  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=5
    !!! Task 6 REJECTED - no worker free and pool is at max
    !!! Task 7 REJECTED - no worker free and pool is at max

core=2, max=5, SynchronousQueue. Does this queue ever hold any waiting tasks in it, even briefly?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new SynchronousQueue<Runnable>());

for (int i = 1; i <= 7; i++) {
    try {
        executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));     // each sleeps 5 seconds
        PoolState.print("accepted task " + i, executor);
    } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
        Log.line("!!! Task " + i + " REJECTED");
    }
}

Actual output:

    accepted task 1     poolSize=1  active=1  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=1
    accepted task 2     poolSize=2  active=2  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=2
    accepted task 3     poolSize=3  active=3  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=3
    accepted task 4     poolSize=4  active=4  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=3
    accepted task 5     poolSize=5  active=5  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=5
    !!! Task 6 REJECTED - no worker free and pool is at max
    !!! Task 7 REJECTED - no worker free and pool is at max

Given SynchronousQueue never accepts a task into storage, what does that force step 2 of the ordering rule ('try the queue') to do on almost every submission once the core is full?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new SynchronousQueue<Runnable>());

for (int i = 1; i <= 7; i++) {
    try {
        executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));     // each sleeps 5 seconds
        PoolState.print("accepted task " + i, executor);
    } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
        Log.line("!!! Task " + i + " REJECTED");
    }
}

Actual output:

    accepted task 1     poolSize=1  active=1  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=1
    accepted task 2     poolSize=2  active=2  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=2
    accepted task 3     poolSize=3  active=3  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=3
    accepted task 4     poolSize=4  active=4  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=3
    accepted task 5     poolSize=5  active=5  queued=0  completed=0  everScheduled=5
    !!! Task 6 REJECTED - no worker free and pool is at max
    !!! Task 7 REJECTED - no worker free and pool is at max

Compare the point at which rejection happens: experiment 4 (ArrayBlockingQueue(3)) accepts 8 tasks before rejecting the 9th. Experiment 11 (SynchronousQueue) accepts only 5 before rejecting the 6th. Why fewer?

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new LinkedBlockingQueue<Runnable>());     // NO capacity given -> unbounded

for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));           // each sleeps 2 seconds
}

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new SynchronousQueue<Runnable>());

for (int i = 1; i <= 7; i++) {
    try {
        executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));     // each sleeps 5 seconds
        PoolState.print("accepted task " + i, executor);
    } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
        Log.line("!!! Task " + i + " REJECTED");
    }
}

Rank these three queue choices from FEWEST extra workers created under sustained heavy load to MOST, all else being equal: (a) unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue, (b) ArrayBlockingQueue(3), (c) SynchronousQueue.

Real code:

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new LinkedBlockingQueue<Runnable>());     // NO capacity given -> unbounded

for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {
    executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));           // each sleeps 2 seconds
}

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        2, 5, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
        new SynchronousQueue<Runnable>());

for (int i = 1; i <= 7; i++) {
    try {
        executor.execute(new SleepTask(i));     // each sleeps 5 seconds
        PoolState.print("accepted task " + i, executor);
    } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
        Log.line("!!! Task " + i + " REJECTED");
    }
